:title("PHP (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a PHP subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

A file starts in HTML MODE: text outside '<?php ... ?>' is literal output (one print
of a constant string), '?>' also stands in for a missing ';', the newline right after
a close tag is swallowed and '<?= e ?>' is shorthand for echo.

The subset matches php-interpreter.abnf: the '<?php' open tag, dollar-sigil variables
(create on first assignment, plus += -= *= /= %= .=), integers, double/single quoted
strings with '$var' / '{$expr}' interpolation, true/false/null, arithmetic and comparison,
the '.' concatenation operator, && || ! and the words and/or, the ternary, prefix/postfix
++ --, if/elseif/else, while, for (;;), foreach ($a as $v) / ($a as $k => $v), functions
with return and DEFAULT parameter values, arrow functions fn($x) => e, array
destructuring (nested, keyed and with holes, in an assignment and in foreach),
closures function($x) use ($y) {...}, class C { public function m() {...} }
with $this->prop / ->method() / new C(...), indexed and associative arrays ([...] and
array(...)), $a[] append, the builtins count/strlen/in_array/array_keys/array_values/
array_push/abs/max/min, and echo/print.

PHP's VALUE MODEL is the one php-interpreter.abnf documents, and the two must agree:
an int is a plain number, a float is BOXED (so 3.0 is not identical to 3 and match(3.0)
picks the float arm), a string is byte based, and an array is ONE ordered key/value map
with a next-automatic-index, so === compares the keys and their order while == does not.
Truthiness is PHP's (false, 0, 0.0, the empty string, the string zero, the empty array
and null are falsy), == is PHP 8 loose equality and the whole operator ladder, the casts
and count/strlen/in_array/abs/max/min go through the js_ph* externals (abnf/jsrtphp.go),
each the twin of the like-named function in the interpreter.

Classes carry class CONSTANTS and STATIC properties in two prefixed key spaces on the
descriptor, both resolved by walking the inheritance chain - so a subclass shares its
parent's single static storage. self:: and static:: live in the CALL FRAME, not in
globals, so the scope-chain snapshot a try restores covers them; static:: is the runtime
class of this, or the class a C::m() call named (late static binding).

Locals, functions, methods and closures lower to the MetaJS handle runtime: scopes via
js_scope_new / js_pyset_var (PHP create-on-assign) / js_scope_get, closures via js_closure
+ js_call, objects/properties via js_obj_new / js_get / js_set, method dispatch via
js_mcall (instances carry a __class descriptor; new C() runs __construct with the instance
bound to $this), arrays via js_pharr / js_phset / js_phpush, indexing via js_phget,
foreach via js_range_len / js_range_key / js_range_val over that same ordered map.
'.' concatenation coerces via js_phcat; && || coerce to booleans with js_phbool. The whole program becomes phpmain(env,
args); a self checking program ends with exit(failures). The same subset is interpreted
directly by php-interpreter.abnf, and the two must agree.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib): identifiers, integers, booleans.
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// PHP whitespace: blanks plus '#' and '//' line comments and '/* */' block comments.
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n" | LineComment | BlockComment } ;
// '#' starts a line comment, but '#[' opens an ATTRIBUTE - the negative lookahead
// keeps the attribute out of the comment. Without it '#[Foo] $p' swallows the rest
// of the line, which is how attributes used to "work" by accident: one written on
// its own line looked like a comment and the declaration under it still parsed,
// while a parameter attribute ate the parameter.
LineComment = ( "//" | HashOnly ) :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HashOnly    = "#" !"[" ;
BlockComment = "/*" :whitespace() { NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStar     = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStarSlash = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\x2e" | "\x30"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// The whole program. A PHP file starts in HTML mode: everything before the first
// '<?php' is literal output, '?>' switches back to HTML mode, and the file may
// alternate any number of times (a file with no '<?php' at all is pure output).
Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = LeadHtml { TopItem } ;
// One item of a top level: a declaration or a statement. Factored out because a
// BRACED namespace body holds exactly the same items (see NsDecl).
TopItem     = Def <~~ push(makeDefStmt(pop())) ~~> | ClassDecl | EnumDecl | ConstDecl | TypeDecl | NsDecl | UseDecl | Statement ;
OpenTag     = "<?php" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// Text before the first open tag. Unlike the '?>' form it keeps a leading newline:
// only the newline directly after a CLOSE tag is swallowed.
LeadHtml    <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), false)) ~~>
            = HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// '?>' leaves PHP mode. Everything up to the next '<?php' (or end of file) is echoed
// verbatim, and the newline immediately after '?>' is swallowed - PHP's own rule, so
// that a template line does not gain a blank line per tag. It is a STATEMENT, which
// is what makes the interleaved template form work inside a body:
//     <?php foreach ($rows as $r): ?><li><?php echo $r; ?></li><?php endforeach; ?>
// '?>' also stands in for a missing ';' (see Semi), exactly as in PHP.
InlineHtml  <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), true)) ~~>
            = "?>" HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// The raw run itself. Whitespace skipping is turned OFF around the scan: the leading
// blanks and newlines of the run are OUTPUT, so they must not be eaten as separators
// before the token the scanner returns is matched.
HtmlText    <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = :whitespace() HtmlScan :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HtmlScan = :script(~~
    (function() {
        // Everything from here to the next opening tag is literal text. '<?php' is
        // matched case-insensitively (PHP's tag is), and '<?=' is the echo shorthand,
        // which ShortEcho then parses as an echo statement.
        var end = 0
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            var b = c.peek(end)
            if (b < 0) { break }
            if (b === 60 && c.peek(end + 1) === 63) {
                if (c.peek(end + 2) === 61) { break }
                var p1 = c.peek(end + 2)
                var h1 = c.peek(end + 3)
                var p2 = c.peek(end + 4)
                if ((p1 === 112 || p1 === 80) && (h1 === 104 || h1 === 72) && (p2 === 112 || p2 === 80)) { break }
            }
            end = end + 1
        }
        // An empty run must SUCCEED matching nothing; returning a zero-length token
        // would not. undefined is the dialect's "match empty".
        if (end === 0) { return undefined }
        // c.peek answers BYTES, so the UTF-8 has to be decoded here or a run holding
        // any non-ASCII text would never match back against the source.
        var out = ""
        var p = 0
        while (p < end) {
            var b0 = c.peek(p)
            if (b0 < 128) { out = out + String.fromCharCode(b0); p = p + 1; continue }
            if (b0 >= 240) {
                var cp4 = ((b0 - 240) * 262144) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 2) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 3) - 128)
                var off = cp4 - 65536
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(55296 + ((off - (off % 1024)) / 1024)) + String.fromCharCode(56320 + (off % 1024))
                p = p + 4
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 224) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 224) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 2) - 128))
                p = p + 3
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 192) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 192) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 1) - 128))
                p = p + 2
                continue
            }
            return abnf.newToken("\x01badutf8", 0)
        }
        return abnf.newToken(out, 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// '<?= e, e ?>' is shorthand for 'echo e, e;'.
ShortEcho   <~~ push(makeEcho(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ShortOpen <~~ push({cmd: "echo"}) ~~> [ Expression { "," Expression } ] [ ";" ] ;
ShortOpen   = "<?=" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A statement terminator. PHP lets a close tag stand in for the ';' ("echo 1 ?>"),
// so every statement that ends in ';' accepts a '?>' AHEAD of it instead - the tag
// itself is left in place and read as the InlineHtml statement that follows.
Semi        = ";" | CloseAhead ;
CloseAhead  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc === 63 && c.peek(i + 1) === 62) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01noclosetag", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A function definition: function name($a, $b) { ... } with explicit return. An
// optional parameter type hint and return type are accepted and ignored (dynamic).
Def         <~~ push(makeFun(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwFunction [ "&" ] Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] Block ;
ParamList   = "(" [ Param { "," Param } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
// A parameter accepts real-world surface: promotion modifiers (public/readonly...),
// a type hint, by-reference '&', variadic '...', and a default value. All but the
// name are accepted and dropped (dynamic, create-on-assign); the default expression
// is parsed then popped so only {param} reaches makeFun.
// A modifier on a constructor parameter is PHP 8's property PROMOTION
// ('__construct(public int $x)' declares and assigns $this->x), so it is captured
// rather than skipped; makeFun turns each promoted parameter into one prologue
// assignment. On any other function a modifier is meaningless and simply ignored.
Param       = [ AttrGrp ] { ParamMod } [ TypeHint ] [ "&" ] [ "..." ] DollarName <~~ push({param: pop()}) ~~> [ ParamDefault ] ;
ParamMod    <~~ push({pmod: up.in}) ~~> = Modifier ;
// A default value is kept: it becomes a small prologue in the emitted body that
// substitutes the default when the argument was not passed (see paramDefaults).
ParamDefault <~~ push({pdef: pop()}) ~~> = PlainEq Expression ;
TypeHint    = [ "?" ] TypeAtom { ( "|" | "&" ) [ "?" ] TypeAtom } ;
TypeAtom    = KwArray | [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
ReturnType  = ":" TypeHint ;

// class C { public function m() {...} } -- method modifiers are accepted and ignored.
ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { ClassMod } KwClass Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ KwExtends ExtName <~~ push({ext: up.in}) ~~> ] [ KwImplements ImplNames ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// The implements list of a real class declaration, captured by name (ImplList itself
// stays item-neutral: interface/trait/enum/anonymous-class all reference it and pop a
// fixed number of items).
ImplNames   = ImplName { "," ImplName } ;
ImplName    <~~ push({impl: up.in}) ~~> = ExtName ;
ClassMod    = ( "abstract" | "final" | "readonly" ) KwEnd ;
ExtName     = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
ImplList    = ExtName { "," ExtName } ;
Member      = MethodDecl | ClassConst | UseTrait | PropDecl ;
// A method: modifiers and a reference-return '&' are accepted and ignored; an
// abstract/interface method has no body (';' instead of a block -> empty method).
MethodDecl  <~~ push({method: makeFun(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { Modifier } KwFunction [ "&" ] MethodName <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] ( Block | ";" ) ;
// Class constants: 'const A = 1, B = 2;', with PHP 8.3's typed form ('const int
// SIZE = 4') and any modifier (final/public/...). They are published on the class
// descriptor under a 'k:' key and resolved by walking __super, so a subclass and an
// implementing class inherit them. An initializer may read 'self::' - makeClassStmt
// binds the class as self BEFORE evaluating them, in declaration order, which is what
// makes 'const TWICE = self::SIZE * 2' work.
ClassConst  <~~ push({cconsts: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { Modifier } KwConst CItem { "," CItem } ";" ;
CItem       <~~ push(makeCItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CIds [ "=" Expression ] ;
// 'const int SIZE' is a TYPED constant: the last word is the name. Each word pushes
// its own record - up.in of the whole run would concatenate them without a separator
// ("intSIZE"), since the capture drops the skipped whitespace.
CIds        = CWord { CWord } ;
CWord       <~~ push({cname: up.in}) ~~> = Id ;
UseTrait    <~~ notImpl("trait use", up.pos); push({prop: true}) ~~>
            = KwUse ImplList ( ";" | SkipBlock ) ;

// interface I extends J, K { const C = 1; public function m(): T; }
// An interface becomes a class descriptor with no method bodies: it carries its
// constants and its parent interfaces, which is exactly what instanceof, a typed
// catch and 'I::CONST' need. A trait has no runtime model here (its members would
// have to be flattened into every using class) and stays notImplemented.
TypeDecl    = IfaceDecl | TraitDecl ;
IfaceDecl   <~~ push(makeIfaceStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwInterface Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ KwExtends ImplNames ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
TraitDecl   <~~ push(notImplStmt("trait declaration", up.pos)) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwTrait SkipHead SkipBlock ;
// namespace N; and the BRACED form namespace N { ... } (and the global 'namespace {}').
// Names are not qualified in this subset, so the declaration itself stays a no-op -
// but the braced body is a top level of its own and its items are simply emitted in
// place, which is what makes a file that wraps everything in 'namespace X { }' work.
NsDecl      <~~ push(makeNs(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwNamespace { Id | "\\" } ( Semi | NsBody ) ;
NsBody      <~~ push({nsbody: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "{" { TopItem } "}" ;
UseDecl     <~~ push(notImplStmt("use import", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwUse { Id | "\\" } [ KwAs Id ] Semi ;
// enum Name : backing-type implements I { case A; ... }: recognized structurally and
// routed to notImplemented; the body (cases, methods, consts) is balanced-skipped.
EnumDecl    <~~ push(notImplStmt("enum", up.pos)) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwEnum Name [ ":" TypeAtom ] [ KwImplements ImplList ] SkipBlock ;
SkipHead    = { Id | "," | "\\" } ;
SkipBlock   = "{" { SkipItem } "}" ;
SkipItem    = SkipDq | SkipSq | SkipBlock | SkipOther ;
SkipDq      = '"' :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipDqChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipSq      = "'" :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipSqChar } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipStrEsc  = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipDqChar  = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipSqChar  = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipOther   = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// A balanced ( ... ) run (switch/match subject, declare/while directive), parsed and
// ignored; string literals inside are matched first so their parens never miscount.
SkipParen   = "(" { SkipDq | SkipSq | SkipParen | SkipPOther } ")" ;
SkipPOther  = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x2a"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// An attribute group '#[Foo(1), Bar]': balanced-skipped wherever PHP allows one.
// Attributes carry no runtime meaning in this subset, so they are recognized and
// dropped rather than routed to notImplemented.
AttrGrp     = Attr { Attr } ;
Attr        = "#[" { AttrItem } "]" ;
AttrItem    = SkipDq | SkipSq | AttrBr | AttrOther ;
AttrBr      = "[" { AttrItem } "]" ;
AttrOther   = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5a" | "\x5c" | "\x5e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// A property declaration 'public $x;' or 'public int $x = e;'. The type hint is
// dropped (dynamic), but the DEFAULT VALUE is kept: makeNew replays the declared
// defaults down the inheritance chain, so "$o->val" reads 7 before anything is
// assigned. A 'static' property is stored on the CLASS descriptor instead (under an
// 's:' key), and because js_phsset walks __super to the declaring class, a subclass
// shares its parent's single storage - PHP's rule.
PropDecl    <~~ push({propdecl: makeProp(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ TypeHint ] DollarName [ "=" Expression ] ( ";" | PropHooks ) ;
// PHP 8.4 property hooks: 'public int $x { get => e; set { ... } }' (and the bodyless
// 'get;' / 'set;' of an abstract or interface property). There is no hook model here,
// so the block is parsed - by the real grammar, not a brace skip, so a '{' inside a
// string cannot miscount - and then DROPPED with a notImplemented note; the property
// itself stays an ordinary declared property.
PropHooks   <~~ takeAll(); notImpl("property hooks", up.pos) ~~>
            = "{" { PropHook } "}" ;
PropHook    = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ "&" ] HookName ( HookArrow | HookBlock | ";" ) ;
HookName    = ( "get" | "set" ) KwEnd ;
HookArrow   = [ ParamList ] "=>" Expression ";" ;
HookBlock   = [ ParamList ] Block ;

PropMod     <~~ push({mod: up.in}) ~~> = Modifier ;
// 'private(set)' / 'protected(set)' is PHP 8.4 asymmetric visibility: the setter is
// more restricted than the getter. Visibility is not enforced in this subset, so the
// '(set)' suffix is simply accepted.
Modifier    = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" | "static" | "final" | "abstract" | "readonly" ) KwEnd [ SetVis ] ;
SetVis      = "(" "set" ")" ;

// Body of a control structure / function: braces around a statement sequence.
Block       <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;

// Every statement pushes exactly one thunk: function(block) -> block.
// A declaration is a statement too: PHP allows `if (...) { class Foo {} }`, and a
// conditionally declared class is common enough in real files to be worth parsing.
Statement   = InlineHtml | ShortEcho | EchoStmt | GlobalStmt | StaticVarStmt | Block | If | While | ForEach | For
            | Switch | DoWhile | Try | Throw | Declare
            | ClassDecl | EnumDecl | TypeDecl | ConstDecl | NsDecl | UseDecl
            | Return | Break | Continue | Empty | ExprStmt ;

// ----- widened statement surface -----
// switch / do-while / declare / static-locals: recognized structurally and routed to
// notImplemented (no runtime model here). Braced bodies are balanced-skipped
// (Switch/Declare) or parsed then dropped (DoWhile) so that a whole real-world file
// parses; under -warn-unsupported each becomes a no-op. (try/catch/finally and throw
// below are genuinely compiled, not notImplemented.)
StaticVarStmt <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplStmt("static local variable", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwStatic DollarName [ "=" Expression ] { "," DollarName [ "=" Expression ] } Semi ;
Switch      <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplStmt("switch", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwSwitch SkipParen ( SkipBlock | SwitchAlt ) ;
SwitchAlt   = ":" { Statement } KwEndswitch Semi ;
DoWhile     <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplStmt("do-while", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwDo Block KwWhile SkipParen Semi ;
// try/catch/finally + throw are genuinely compiled: throw calls js_throw, each try/catch/
// finally body compiles to its own closure handed to js_try, and a return/break/continue
// leaving a try or catch body becomes a control signal that excDispatch re-issues in the
// enclosing frame. Every catch clause is compiled and the first MATCHING one wins: the
// type list (including the multi-catch A | B) is tested with js_phcatch, and a value no
// clause matches is re-thrown. The catch variable is optional. A PHP Block is
// {body: thunk}, so the marked sub-rules unwrap it (pop().body).
Try         <~~ push(makeTry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTry TryBody { CatchClause } [ FinallyClause ] ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop().body}) ~~>
            = Block ;
CatchClause <~~ push(makeCatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCatch "(" CatchType [ DollarName ] ")" Block <~~ push(pop().body) ~~> ;
FinallyClause <~~ push({finbody: pop().body}) ~~>
            = KwFinally Block ;
Throw       <~~ push(makeThrow(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression Semi ;
Declare     <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplStmt("declare", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwDeclare SkipParen ( ";" | Block ) ;
ConstDecl   <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplStmt("global const", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwConst Id { Id } [ "=" Expression ] { "," Id { Id } [ "=" Expression ] } Semi ;
CatchType   = CatchName { "|" CatchName } ;
CatchName   <~~ push({ctype: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = ExtName ;

// echo e, e, e;  /  print e;
EchoStmt    <~~ push(makeEcho(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwEcho <~~ push({cmd: up.in}) ~~> [ Expression { "," Expression } ] Semi ;

// global $a, $b; -- in this subset function bodies already resolve names through
// the enclosing (global) scope chain, so 'global' is accepted as a no-op.
GlobalStmt  <~~ takeAll(); push(makeConstStmt()) ~~>
            = KwGlobal DollarName { "," DollarName } Semi ;

Empty       <~~ push(makeConstStmt()) ~~>
            = ";" ;

ExprStmt    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression Semi ;

// Both spellings of every compound statement: braces, and PHP's colon/endXXX form.
// The alternative form is tried FIRST because it is decided by a single ':' - it is
// AltBody that then has to be careful: a bare 'endif' would otherwise parse as a
// constant-reference statement and swallow the terminator, which is why the five
// endXXX words are reserved in NotKw.
If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" ( AltIfTail | Block { ElseIf } [ KwElse Block <~~ push({els: pop().body}) ~~> ] ) ;
ElseIf      <~~ var b = pop(); push({elifCond: pop(), elifBody: b.body}) ~~>
            = KwElseif "(" Expression ")" Block ;
AltIfTail   = ":" AltBody { AltElseIf } [ KwElse ":" AltBody <~~ push({els: pop().body}) ~~> ] KwEndif Semi ;
AltElseIf   <~~ var b2 = pop(); push({elifCond: pop(), elifBody: b2.body}) ~~>
            = KwElseif "(" Expression ")" ":" AltBody ;
// A statement run that ends at the enclosing endXXX / else / elseif keyword. It
// pushes the same {body} record a Block does, so every builder is reused unchanged.
AltBody     <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = { Statement } ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(pop().body, pop())) ~~>
            = KwWhile "(" Expression ")" ( AltWhileBody | Block | BodyStmt ) ;
AltWhileBody = ":" AltBody KwEndwhile Semi ;

// for (init; cond; post) { body }. The three clauses are optional and wrapped
// with a kind marker so the builder can tell them apart on the stack.
For         <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" [ ForInit ] ";" [ ForCond ] ";" [ ForPost ] ")" ( AltForBody | Block | BodyStmt ) ;
AltForBody  = ":" AltBody KwEndfor Semi ;
ForInit     <~~ push({k: "init", t: exprToStmt(pop())}) ~~> = Expression ;
ForCond     <~~ push({k: "cond", t: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
ForPost     <~~ push({k: "post", t: exprToStmt(pop())}) ~~> = Expression ;

// foreach ($arr as $v) / foreach ($arr as $k => $v)
ForEach     <~~ push(makeForeach(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwForeach "(" Expression KwAs ForeachVars ")" ( AltForeachBody | Block | BodyStmt ) ;
AltForeachBody = ":" AltBody KwEndforeach Semi ;
// 'as [$k, $v]' destructures each element; the pattern is the same one list()/[] uses
// in an assignment, so both spellings bind identically.
ForeachVars = DestructPat | ForeachKV ;
ForeachKV   = ForeachV1 [ "=>" ForeachV2 ] ;
ForeachV1   = [ FeRef ] DollarName <~~ push({v1: pop()}) ~~> ;
ForeachV2   = [ FeRef ] DollarName <~~ push({v2: pop()}) ~~> ;
// 'as &$v' aliases the array ELEMENT, so writing $v writes back into the array.
// There is no reference cell in this compiler's value model, so the form is
// recognized and routed to notImplemented rather than compiled as a copy.
FeRef       <~~ notImpl("foreach by reference", up.pos) ~~> = "&" ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwReturn [ Expression ] Semi ;
Break       <~~ push(makeBreak(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwBreak [ Expression ] Semi ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinue(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwContinue [ Expression ] Semi ;

// Every expression pushes exactly one thunk: function(block) -> {b, v}.
Expression  = RefAssign | DestructAssign | Assignment | Ternary ;

// $b = &$a : both names then share ONE storage cell. There is no reference cell in
// this compiler's value model, so the form is recognized and routed to
// notImplemented rather than silently compiled as a copy.
RefAssign   <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplExpr("reference assignment", up.pos)) ~~>
            = Target PlainEq "&" !"&" Target ;

// list($a, $b) = e  /  [$a, $b] = e  -- array destructuring. A slot may be EMPTY
// ([, $second]), KEYED (["y" => $py]) or itself a nested pattern ([[$m, $n], [$o]]).
// Tried before a plain expression so a bracketed target list is not mistaken for an
// array literal; a real array literal still rolls back here because the '=' never
// follows. The shape is the interpreter's, so the two agree slot for slot.
DestructAssign <~~ push(makeDestructure(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DestructPat PlainEq Expression ;
DestructPat <~~ push({pat: makePattern(takeAll())}) ~~>
